\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 \ul \ulc0 Setting up CloudWatch Alarms & Logs\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs26 \cf0 \ulnone \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 {\listtext	0.	}Log in to the 
\f0\b AWS Management Console
\f1\b0  and open the 
\f0\b CloudWatch
\f1\b0  console.\
{\listtext	0.	}In the left navigation pane, choose 
\f0\b Alarms
\f1\b0  and then click 
\f0\b All alarms
\f1\b0 .\
{\listtext	0.	}Click the 
\f0\b Create alarm
\f1\b0  button.\
{\listtext	0.	}Click 
\f0\b Select metric
\f1\b0 . This will open a menu where you can browse all available metrics (EC2, Lambda, RDS, etc.).\
{\listtext	0.	}Choose a category (e.g., 
\f0\b EC2 > Per-Instance Metrics
\f1\b0 ), check the box next to the metric you want (e.g., CPUUtilization), and click 
\f0\b Select metric
\f1\b0 .\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\sa40\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf0 Step 2: Specify Metric and Conditions\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs26 \cf0 This is where you define what "bad" looks like.\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f2\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Metric Name/Instance ID:
\f1\b0  Double-check that you've selected the right resource.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f2\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Statistic:
\f1\b0  Usually set to 
\f0\b Average
\f1\b0 , but you might want 
\f0\b Maximum
\f1\b0  if you're looking for spikes.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f2\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Period:
\f1\b0  How often the metric is evaluated (e.g., every 5 minutes).\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f2\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Threshold Type:
\f1\b0  * 
\f0\b Static:
\f1\b0  You define a fixed number (e.g., "Greater than 80").\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl1
\f2\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Anomaly Detection:
\f1\b0  AWS uses ML to determine the threshold based on past behavior.\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f2\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Conditions:
\f1\b0  Define the logic (e.g., Whenever CPUUtilization is Greater/Threshold than 80 for 1 out of 1 datapoints).\
\pard\pardeftab560\sa40\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf0 \
Step 3: Configure Actions (Notifications)\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs26 \cf0 Now, tell AWS what to do when the alarm state is 
\f0\b In alarm
\f1\b0 .\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf0 {\listtext	0.	}
\f0\b Alarm state trigger:
\f1\b0  Select 
\f0\b In alarm
\f1\b0 .\
{\listtext	0.	}
\f0\b Send a notification to:
\f1\b0  Select an existing 
\f0\b SNS Topic
\f1\b0  or click 
\f0\b Create new topic
\f1\b0 .\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl1
\f2\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\fs26 If creating a new one, enter an email address where you want to receive the alert.\
\ls3\ilvl1
\f2\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f3\i\fs26 Note: You must click the "Confirm Subscription" link in the email AWS sends you for this to work.
\f1\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf0 {\listtext	0.	}
\f0\b Auto Scaling/EC2 Actions (Optional):
\f1\b0  You can also tell the alarm to reboot the server or scale your group if the threshold is hit.\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab560\sa40\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf0 Step 4: Add Name and Description\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs26 \cf0 Give your alarm a clear, descriptive name so you know exactly what\'92s happening when you get a text at 2:00 AM.\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f2\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Example Name:
\f1\b0  Production-Web-Server-High-CPU-Alarm\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f2\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Description:
\f1\b0  Triggers when CPU exceeds 80% for 5 minutes.\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\sa40\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf0 Step 5: Preview and Create\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs26 \cf0 {\listtext	0.	}Review all your settings on the final summary page.\
{\listtext	0.	}Check the 
\f0\b Preview graph
\f1\b0  to see how your historical data would have interacted with this new alarm.\
{\listtext	0.	}Click 
\f0\b Create alarm
\f1\b0 .\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
